1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,749
Lesson 24: tolerances, cable and balun, crossed Yagis, dividers and H-frame

2
00:00:05,799 --> 00:00:17,376
YA-NEC course, lesson 24: the VHF tools of 1.2. From the Examples we open the 10-element DJ9BV Yagi for 144 MHz.

3
00:00:17,426 --> 00:00:28,698
Patterns, Tolerances item: construction errors on element length, position on the boom and diameter, with the Monte Carlo method.

4
00:00:28,748 --> 00:00:36,972
We set the trials to 100 and press Start: each trial is a Yagi with random errors, calculated with NEC.

5
00:00:37,022 --> 00:00:48,349
The result: nominal F/B 19 dB, 18.1 in the worst 5% of cases. SWR below 1.52 in 95% of the trials.

6
00:00:48,399 --> 00:00:59,216
On the right the sensitivity per element; below the cutting list with the allowed tolerance: in red the critical elements, to be cut more carefully.

7
00:00:59,266 --> 00:01:20,060
Now the 7-element DK7ZB Yagi. In the 3D structure, among the model objects, Cable and balun: a large sheet opens.

8
00:01:20,110 --> 00:01:27,670
The cable shield and the mast become NEC wires. Calculate the comparison between no balun and the VHF baluns.

9
00:01:27,720 --> 00:01:39,775
Without balun 15% of the dipole current flows on the shield and the F/B drops to 19.6 dB. With the lambda/4 sleeve: 3% and 23.8 dB.

10
00:01:39,825 --> 00:01:48,308
Ferrites work, but at 1 kW they dissipate about 31 W: the chart shows the current along the shield for each case.

11
00:01:48,358 --> 00:01:59,567
Calculators, VHF, Crossed Yagis: two identical Yagis in X or +, with the planes offset by lambda/4 on the boom.

12
00:01:59,617 --> 00:02:07,749
Switching: horizontal, vertical, right or left circular, X-pol for EME. We press Calculate with right-hand circular.

13
00:02:07,799 --> 00:02:19,599
RHCP 13.3 dBic, axial ratio 0 dB on axis and 0.3 dB at -3 dB; below, axial ratio and SWR versus frequency.

14
00:02:19,649 --> 00:02:36,290
Last example: the stack of 4 DK7ZB Yagis on an H-frame. Calculators, Dividers and phasing lines.

15
00:02:36,340 --> 00:02:48,962
Lambda/4 divider in square tube: Z0 25 ohm, inner conductor 14.2 mm, about 512 mm to cut. Dimensioned drawing, printable 1:1.

16
00:02:49,012 --> 00:02:58,829
Connected to the Stack, the input SWR uses the active impedances of the four antennas; on the right the phasing lines, equal lengths.

17
00:02:58,879 --> 00:03:12,695
3D structure, Stack object: at the bottom, H-frame and mast in the stack.

18
00:03:12,745 --> 00:03:20,552
Uprights, crossbar, mast and phasing-line shields enter the calculation as NEC wires. Calculate comparison.

19
00:03:20,602 --> 00:03:34,315
With the frame: gain -0.4 dB, average F/R -3.8 dB, G/T -0.5 dB/K. With horizontal polarization the crossbar and the phasing lines matter.

20
00:03:34,365 --> 00:03:45,227
Sources: DL6WU, DK7ZB, W8NX, ON4UN, ARRL Antenna Book, VE7BQH. All in the manual, chapter 23.
